response.value:
The eating check happens **before** you add the new head, so it compares the *old* head position with the food. By the time the new head reaches the food, you’ve already removed the tail—so the snake stays the same length.

Calculate the next head first, then check whether **that position** matches the food. Only remove the tail if it doesn’t:

```js
const nextHead = { x: snake[0].x + direction.x, y: snake[0].y + direction.y };
const eating = nextHead.x === food.x && nextHead.y === food.y;
if (!eating) snake.pop();
snake.unshift(nextHead);
```

Then use `eating` for the food-relocation logic too, so both checks refer to the same move.
